  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nMonday, Hurricane Polo is expected to take on a more northeasterly \ncourse crossing into the Baja Peninsula. Polo is expected to weaken \nas it interacts with the upper level trough, becoming integrated \ninto the upper level flow. This will help bring yet another plume of \ndeeper moisture across the Desert Southwest into the Great Plains. \nHowever, guidance continues to struggle with integrating the \nhurricane into the jet stream, which continues to add uncertainty \ninto the location and timing of precipitation early to mid week. For \nnow, the general consensus in the deterministic models shows \nintegration sometime on Tuesday. However, a look at the GEFS \nspaghetti plots shows some slightly faster solutions. In fact, the \ndisagreement remains readily visible in spaghetti plots, as there is \na fair amount of divergence in solutions by Tuesday afternoon. All \nthis to say, forecast confidence remains low to medium beyond \nTuesday, especially in placement and timing of precipitation, as \nthere is still a fairly wide spread in solutions. However, there are \nsome forecast trends that are gaining higher confidence, giving \nslightly higher confidence in the weather pattern throughout the \nweek. \n\nFor Monday, the upper level trough is expected to track east, which \nshould continue to increase rain chances Monday afternoon and Monday \nnight. As previously mentioned, the remnants of Hurricane Odalys is \nexpected to be integrated into the flow, bringing a strong plume of \ntropical moisture into the Great Plains. Ensemble guidance continues \nto highlight increasing chances for rainfall Monday night, including \npotential for locally heavy rainfall across western and north \ncentral Nebraska. The European ensembles bring this plume of \nmoisture into the region a slightly more quickly that the GEFS \nensembles, now indicating a 40 to 50 percent chance of exceeding \nhalf an inch of liquid precipitation Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning. However, while the GEFS only brings a 10 percent chance of \nthis occurring, it does show a 70 to 80 percent chance of rainfall \noccurring. THis is also highlighted by the latest NBM deterministic, \nso made no changes to precipitation chances Monday evening into \nTuesday morning. \n\nBy Tuesday, as Polo becomes integrated into the jet stream, \nexpecting a plume of moisture from that system to track into the \nPlains as well. This will continue to bring chances for rain, \nespecially a potential for heavy rainfall. At this time, the greater \nchances for heavy rainfall remain to our south and east; however, \nthere is still some potential for this deeper layer of moisture \nacross portions of our area, especially across southwest into central \nNebraska. With this push of deeper moisture, ensemble guidance \nsuggests anywhere from a 20 to 50 percent chance (with the more \nprogressive European solutions being on the higher end)of total \nrainfall exceeding one inch across southwest into central Nebraska \nMonday through Thursday afternoon. Again, a lot of the timing \nquestions remain given the poor integration of Hurricane Polo into \nthe jet stream, but precipitation chance do remain fairly likely \nduring this period. \n\nThursday into Friday, the upper level low is expected to track \nacross the southern Plains, potentially lifting north across the \nregion. On the back end of this system, expecting precipitation\nchances to wane late week into the weekend. With the upper \nlevel low tracking potentially into the region, expecting some \nslightly cooler temperatures to prevail across the region, with \nhighs in the upper 60s to lower 70s. This would not be too \nunusual for this time of year, as typical highs to start October\nare around 72 degrees across western and north central \nNebraska.",
